-- Hero honoree bios corrected to CrossFit's published bios (2026-11-22).
do $$
declare n int;
begin
  update benchmark_workouts set faqs = '[{"answer": "Sub-20 minutes is elite. 25–35 minutes is solid. Kalsu is widely considered one of the most brutal benchmark WODs — any finish is a victory.", "question": "What is a good Kalsu time?"}, {"answer": "Start with 5 burpees, then at the top of every new minute perform 5 burpees before resuming thrusters. The workout ends when you reach 100 total thrusters.", "question": "How does the Kalsu format work?"}, {"answer": "Kalsu honors U.S. Army 1st Lt. James Robert \"Bob\" Kalsu, a guard for the Buffalo Bills who was killed on July 21, 1970, at Fire Support Base Ripcord in Vietnam while serving with the 101st Airborne Division.", "question": "Who was Kalsu?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'kalsu';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'kalsu'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Loredo honors U.S. Army Staff Sergeant Edwardo Loredo, 34, of Houston, Texas, who was killed on June 24, 2010, in Jelewar, Afghanistan, when insurgents attacked his unit with an improvised explosive device. SSgt. Loredo served with the 2nd Battalion, 508th Parachute Infantry Regiment, 4th Brigade Combat Team, 82nd Airborne Division, based at Fort Bragg, North Carolina. He is survived by his wife, 1st Sgt. Jennifer Loredo; his daughter, Laura Isabelle; his stepdaughter, Alexis; and his son, Eddie Enrique.

Six rounds of 24 air squats, 24 push-ups, 24 walking lunges, and a 400m run — entirely bodyweight, entirely demanding. The repetitive lower-body volume (24 squats + 24 lunges per round = 48 leg movements) creates significant quad and hip fatigue that compounds with each run. The 24 push-ups provide upper-body demand while the lower body rests slightly; the 400m run returns leg demand immediately after the lunges. By round 4, the air squats feel much heavier than they did in round 1. The workout is accessible to any athlete but punishing for all of them. Sub-40 minutes is a strong performance.', faqs = '[{"answer": "U.S. Army Staff Sergeant Edwardo Loredo, 34, of Houston, Texas, killed on June 24, 2010, in Jelewar, Afghanistan, when insurgents attacked his unit with an improvised explosive device. He served with the 2nd Battalion, 508th Parachute Infantry Regiment, 82nd Airborne Division.", "question": "Who was Edwardo Loredo?"}, {"answer": "Sub-40 minutes is strong. Sub-35 minutes is advanced. Most athletes finish in 35-50 minutes.", "question": "What is a good time for Loredo?"}, {"answer": "24 air squats + 24 walking lunges = 48 leg movements per round x 6 rounds = 288 total leg reps, plus 2.4km of running.", "question": "How many total leg reps are in Loredo?"}, {"answer": "No — Loredo is entirely bodyweight. You need a 400m running route and enough space for walking lunges.", "question": "Is any equipment needed for Loredo?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'loredo';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'loredo'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Lumberjack 20 honors the victims of the Nov. 5, 2009, attack at Fort Hood, Texas, where a gunman killed 12 soldiers and one civilian and wounded 43 others. Four of the soldiers killed, Spc. Frederick Greene, Pfc. Aaron Thomas Nemelka, Pfc. Michael Pearson and Spc. Kham Xiong, along with 11 of the wounded, were active CrossFit athletes in the 20th Engineer Battalion, home to Lumberjack CrossFit. The workout is seven sets of 20 reps, each followed by a 400m run: deadlifts (275 lb), KB swings (70 lb), overhead squats (115 lb), burpees, chest-to-bar pull-ups, box jumps, and dumbbell squat cleans (45 lb each), with 2,800 total meters of running woven throughout.

This is one of the longest and most varied hero WODs in the library, designed to touch every aspect of fitness while demanding pacing across a 60+ minute effort. The heavy deadlifts open the workout when athletes are freshest; the chipper progresses through increasingly aerobic movements. The seven 400m runs serve as both tribute and transition — a chance to breathe and reset between demanding stations. Completing Lumberjack 20 is a meaningful physical and emotional experience.', faqs = '[{"answer": "The victims of the Nov. 5, 2009, attack at Fort Hood, Texas, which killed 12 soldiers and one civilian. Four of the soldiers killed, Spc. Frederick Greene, Pfc. Aaron Thomas Nemelka, Pfc. Michael Pearson and Spc. Kham Xiong, were CrossFit athletes in the 20th Engineer Battalion, home to Lumberjack CrossFit.", "question": "Who does Lumberjack 20 honor?"}, {"answer": "Each of the seven movements is a set of 20 reps followed by a 400m run, creating a chipper with running transitions. The workout is named for the 20th Engineer Battalion and its gym, Lumberjack CrossFit.", "question": "Why are there 7 movements and 7 runs in Lumberjack 20?"}, {"answer": "60-90 minutes for most athletes. This is one of the longest hero WODs. Sub-60 minutes is a strong performance.", "question": "How long does Lumberjack 20 take?"}, {"answer": "7 x 400m = 2,800m (approximately 1.75 miles) interspersed between the seven movement stations.", "question": "What is the total running distance in Lumberjack 20?"}]'::jsonb, scaling_notes = 'Scale deadlift to 185-225 lb. Scale KB swing to 53/35 lb. Scale OHS to 75-95 lb. Scale DB squat clean to 35 lb each. Keep all seven movements and all seven runs.', updated_at = now() where slug = 'lumberjack-20';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'lumberjack-20'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Rankel honors Sergeant John Rankel, United States Marine Corps, 23, of Speedway, Indiana, who was killed on June 7, 2010, while supporting combat operations in Helmand Province, Afghanistan. Sgt. Rankel was assigned to the 3rd Battalion, 1st Marine Regiment, 1st Marine Division. He is survived by his mother and stepfather, his father and stepmother, and his brothers.

The Rankel workout is a 20-minute AMRAP of 6 deadlifts (225/155 pounds), 7 burpee pull-ups, 10 kettlebell swings (70/53 pounds), and a 200-meter run. It is a full-body chipper AMRAP that combines strength, gymnastics, and cardio across one of the longer hero WOD time domains.

The deadlift weight is moderate but will accumulate. Six reps is a manageable set, but across 20 minutes of AMRAPs, the barbell will demand respect. Burpee pull-ups combine two movements into one — the burpee provides the momentum for the pull-up, and most athletes can string them together efficiently when fresh.

The 70-pound kettlebell swings are heavier than standard — especially combined with the deadlifts — making posterior chain endurance the critical factor in this workout. The 200-meter run serves as a brief cardio reset between rounds. Keep a pace on the run that allows you to step back to the barbell ready to work.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Rankel honors Sergeant John Rankel, USMC, 23, of Speedway, Indiana, who was killed on June 7, 2010, while supporting combat operations in Helmand Province, Afghanistan.", "question": "Who is Rankel named after?"}, {"answer": "A burpee pull-up combines a standard burpee with a pull-up at the top. After jumping up at the end of the burpee, you grab the pull-up bar and complete one pull-up before dropping down for the next rep.", "question": "What is a burpee pull-up?"}, {"answer": "70 lb (32 kg) is heavier than the typical 53 lb (24 kg) used in most workouts. This is intentional in Rankel — the heavier bell increases the posterior chain demand significantly.", "question": "Is 70 lb a standard kettlebell weight?"}, {"answer": "For intermediate athletes, 5–7 rounds in 20 minutes is a solid benchmark. Competitive athletes may complete 8–10+ rounds.", "question": "How many rounds is a good score for Rankel?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'rankel';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'rankel'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Ryan honors Maplewood, Missouri, firefighter Ryan Hummert, 22, who was killed by sniper fire on July 21, 2008, when he stepped off his fire truck while responding to a call. He is survived by his parents, Andrew and Jackie.

The Ryan workout is five rounds for time of 7 muscle-ups and 21 burpees. It is a masterclass in upper-body pulling under cardiovascular stress — combining one of the most demanding gymnastics skills with the most cardiovascular bodyweight movement.

Seven muscle-ups per round means 35 total muscle-ups alongside 105 burpees. For athletes who are proficient on rings, the muscle-up fatigue will build steadily across rounds — the false grip, the transition, and the lockout all degrade under accumulated effort. The burpees amplify shoulder fatigue by elevating heart rate and demanding push-up strength immediately before the next set of muscle-ups.

The key to Ryan is managing the muscle-up sets. In round one, go unbroken if possible to build your round. By round three, it is smarter to go 4+3 or 3+2+2 than to miss reps and rest longer than planned. Burpees should be done at a metronomic pace — consistent, not fast — to protect your capacity for the next muscle-up set.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Ryan honors Maplewood, Missouri, firefighter Ryan Hummert, 22, who was killed by sniper fire on July 21, 2008, when he stepped off his fire truck while responding to a call.", "question": "Who is Ryan named after?"}, {"answer": "Ryan totals 140 reps: 35 muscle-ups and 105 burpees across five rounds.", "question": "What are the total reps in Ryan?"}, {"answer": "Scale to ring rows + ring dips (7 of each) or jumping muscle-ups. The goal is to maintain a pulling + pressing movement that challenges your gymnastics capacity.", "question": "What if I can''t do muscle-ups?"}, {"answer": "Athletes with solid muscle-ups typically finish in 20–35 minutes. With scaling, plan for 25–40 minutes depending on your gymnastics proficiency.", "question": "How long does Ryan take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'ryan';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'ryan'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Griff honors U.S. Air Force Staff Sergeant Travis L. Griffin, 28, who was killed on April 3, 2008, in the Rasheed District of Baghdad, Iraq, when an improvised explosive device struck his vehicle. He is survived by his son, Elijah.

A deceptively simple running workout: 800m forward run, 400m backwards run, 800m forward run, 400m backwards run. No barbell, no gymnastics — just 2.4 kilometers of running with half the distance done in reverse. The backwards running is the defining challenge: it taxes your calves, hamstrings, and coordination in ways forward running does not. Balance and proprioception are genuinely tested, especially as fatigue builds in the second 400m backwards segment. Griff is a workout that rewards athletes who train multiple planes of movement and punishes those who have only ever run forward.', faqs = '[{"answer": "U.S. Air Force Staff Sergeant Travis L. Griffin, 28, killed on April 3, 2008, in the Rasheed District of Baghdad, Iraq, when an improvised explosive device struck his vehicle.", "question": "Who was Travis Griffin?"}, {"answer": "Sub-20 minutes is strong. Sub-16 minutes is elite. Most athletes finish in 18-25 minutes depending on backwards running experience.", "question": "What is a good time for Griff?"}, {"answer": "Backwards running reverses the primary propulsive muscles — calves and hamstrings take the brunt rather than quads and hip flexors. It also demands coordination and balance that forward running does not.", "question": "Why is backwards running so challenging?"}, {"answer": "800m + 400m + 800m + 400m = 2,400m total (1,600m forward, 800m backwards).", "question": "What is the total distance in Griff?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'griff';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'griff'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Manion honors U.S. Marine Corps 1st Lieutenant Travis Manion, 26, of Doylestown, Pennsylvania, who was assigned to the 1st Reconnaissance Battalion, 1st Marine Division. He was killed by sniper fire on April 29, 2007, while fighting against an enemy ambush in Anbar Province, Iraq, and was posthumously awarded the Silver Star and the Bronze Star with Valor. His motto, "If Not Me, Then Who...", has become a rallying cry for veterans and civilians alike.

Seven rounds of a 400m run and 29 back squats (135/95 lb) — 203 total back squats and 2,800 meters of running. The 29 reps match April 29, the date he was killed. The back squat weight is moderate but relentless across 7 rounds; the run fatigues the legs just in time for more squats. By round 4, the squat sets feel significantly heavier than round 1. Even pacing is the strategic challenge — athletes who go out hard find rounds 5-7 punishing. Sub-35 minutes is a strong performance.', faqs = '[{"answer": "USMC 1st Lt. Travis Manion, 26, of the 1st Reconnaissance Battalion, killed by sniper fire on April 29, 2007, while fighting against an enemy ambush in Anbar Province, Iraq. He was posthumously awarded the Silver Star. His motto ''If Not Me, Then Who...'' is carried by veterans worldwide.", "question": "Who was Travis Manion?"}, {"answer": "Travis Manion was 26 when he was killed. CrossFit does not explain the number, but 29 matches April 29, the date of his death.", "question": "Why 29 reps in Manion?"}, {"answer": "Under 35 minutes is strong.", "question": "What is a good time for Manion?"}, {"answer": "135 lb for men, 95 lb for women — the same weight across all 7 rounds.", "question": "What weight is the back squat in Manion?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'manion';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'manion'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set faqs = '[{"answer": "Sub-8 min is strong.", "question": "What is a good Randy time?"}, {"answer": "75# for men, 55# for women at Rx. If you cannot do 15 unbroken power snatches when fresh at that weight, scale down to preserve form across all 75 reps.", "question": "What weight should I use for Randy?"}, {"answer": "Randy honors Randy Simmons, 51, a 27-year LAPD veteran and SWAT team member who was killed in the line of duty on Feb. 7, 2008. It is a hero benchmark WOD honoring his service.", "question": "Who was Randy?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'randy';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'randy'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Hansen honors U.S. Marine Corps Staff Sergeant Daniel Hansen, who died on February 14, 2009, in Farah Province, Afghanistan, when an improvised explosive device he was working on detonated. He is survived by his mother, Sheryll; his father, Delbert; his younger sister, Katie; and his twin brother, Matthew, also a Marine.

Five rounds of 30 kettlebell swings (70/53 lb), 30 burpees, and 30 GHD sit-ups — 90 reps per round, 450 total, with no running to break things up. The heavy kettlebell (2-pood) demands full hip extension on every swing; the 30 burpees that follow provide metabolic stress; the GHD sit-ups close each round with a core and hip flexor demand that, if you are not accustomed to GHD volume, can leave you with severe delayed-onset muscle soreness. This is one of the most volume-dense hero WODs. Sub-40 minutes is a strong benchmark.', faqs = '[{"answer": "USMC Staff Sergeant Daniel Hansen, who died on February 14, 2009, in Farah Province, Afghanistan, when an improvised explosive device he was working on detonated.", "question": "Who was Daniel Hansen?"}, {"answer": "Sub-40 minutes is strong. Sub-35 minutes is advanced. This is a high-volume workout — most athletes finish in 35-55 minutes.", "question": "What is a good time for Hansen?"}, {"answer": "70 lb (2 pood) for men, 53 lb (1.5 pood) for women.", "question": "How heavy is the kettlebell in Hansen?"}, {"answer": "Thirty GHD sit-ups per round x 5 rounds = 150 total. Athletes unaccustomed to GHD volume can experience severe DOMS (muscle soreness that peaks 2-3 days later). Scale the reps if you haven''t trained GHD recently.", "question": "Why is the GHD sit-up scaling so important in Hansen?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'hansen';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'hansen'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Daniel honors U.S. Army Sgt. 1st Class Daniel Crabtree, who was killed in Al Kut, Iraq, on June 8, 2006. He is survived by his wife, Kathy, and his daughter, Mallory. It is a chipper workout that mirrors the structure of sustained effort: 50 pull-ups, a 400m run, 21 thrusters (95/65 lb), an 800m run, 21 more thrusters, another 400m run, and 50 pull-ups to close. The bookending pull-up sets (50 at the start, 50 at the end) create a symmetrical demand — the first set is a sprint, the second arrives after 42 thrusters and 1,600m of running and feels entirely different.

The workout is a test of pacing and mental toughness. Athletes who race the opening 50 pull-ups and first thruster set slow dramatically in the back half. Those who pace all six stations find that the closing 50 pull-ups, while hard, are achievable. The 800m center run is the pivot point — pushing it hard costs you on the second thruster set and final pull-ups. Sub-25 minutes is a strong performance.', updated_at = now() where slug = 'daniel';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'daniel'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Erin honors Canadian Army Master Corporal Erin Melvin Doyle, 32, of the 3rd Battalion, Princess Patricia''s Canadian Light Infantry, who was killed in a firefight on August 11, 2008, in the Panjwaii District of Kandahar Province, Afghanistan. He is survived by his wife, Nicole, and his daughter, Zarine.

Five rounds of 15 dumbbell split cleans (40 lb each / 30 lb each) and 21 pull-ups. The dumbbell split clean is a technically demanding movement — dropping into a split position while cleaning two dumbbells simultaneously requires hip flexibility, balance, and coordination. Fifteen reps per round with alternating legs creates 75 split cleans across the workout. Combined with 105 pull-ups, the grip and shoulder demand is relentless. The split clean taxes legs differently than a standard clean, and the accumulated hip flexor and quad fatigue compounds as rounds progress. Sub-25 minutes is a solid performance.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Canadian Army Master Corporal Erin Melvin Doyle, 32, of the 3rd Battalion, Princess Patricia''s Canadian Light Infantry, killed in a firefight on August 11, 2008, in the Panjwaii District of Kandahar Province, Afghanistan.", "question": "Who was Erin Doyle?"}, {"answer": "A clean performed with two dumbbells where you drop into a split (lunge) position to receive the weight at the top of the pull. One foot steps forward, one back, and both bells are cleaned simultaneously to the shoulders.", "question": "What is a dumbbell split clean?"}, {"answer": "Sub-25 minutes is solid. The 75 split cleans and 105 pull-ups make this a grip-intensive workout with a typical finish time of 20-35 minutes.", "question": "What is a good time for Erin?"}, {"answer": "40 lb each hand for men, 30 lb each hand for women.", "question": "What dumbbell weight is used in Erin?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'erin';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'erin'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Glen honors former U.S. Navy SEAL Glen Doherty, 42, of Winchester, Massachusetts, who was assigned to a State Department security detail in Benghazi, Libya, and died in an attack on the U.S. consulate there on September 11, 2012. He is survived by his parents, Ben and Barbara; his sister, Katie; and his brother, Greg.

A long-duration chipper: 30 clean-and-jerks (135/95 lb), 1 mile run, 10 rope climbs (15 ft), 1 mile run, and 100 burpees. The five stations hit every aspect of fitness — Olympic lifting, aerobic running, upper-body pulling and climbing, and metabolic conditioning. The 2-mile total run separates the barbell and rope work from the burpee finale, making Glen one of the most well-rounded hero WODs in the library. Most athletes take 45-70 minutes to complete it. Finishing under 60 minutes is a meaningful benchmark.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Glen Doherty, 42, was a former Navy SEAL assigned to a State Department security detail in Benghazi, Libya. He died in an attack on the U.S. consulate there on September 11, 2012.", "question": "Who was Glen Doherty?"}, {"answer": "Sub-60 min is a solid goal. Finishing at any time is a meaningful achievement — this is one of the longer hero WODs.", "question": "What is a good time for Glen?"}, {"answer": "30 clean-and-jerks (135/95 lb) → 1 mile run → 10 rope climbs (15 ft) → 1 mile run → 100 burpees.", "question": "What are all the movements in Glen?"}, {"answer": "45-70 minutes for most athletes. The combination of 2 miles of running, 30 heavy C&Js, 10 rope climbs, and 100 burpees makes this one of the more time-consuming hero WODs.", "question": "How long does Glen typically take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'glen';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'glen'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Weaver honors First Lieutenant Todd W. Weaver, United States Army, 26, of Hampton, Virginia, who died on September 9, 2010, of wounds suffered when insurgents attacked his unit with a roadside bomb in Kandahar, Afghanistan. 1LT Weaver was assigned to the 1st Battalion, 320th Field Artillery Regiment, 2nd Brigade Combat Team, 101st Airborne Division (Air Assault), Fort Campbell, Kentucky. He is survived by his wife, Emma; his daughter, Kiley; his parents; and his siblings.

The Weaver workout is four rounds for time of 10 L-pull-ups, 15 push-ups, 15 chest-to-bar pull-ups, 15 push-ups, 20 pull-ups, and 15 push-ups — with only push-ups between each successive pull-up variation. The total in each round is 45 pull-ups (in three varieties) and 45 push-ups, for a grand total of 180 pull-ups and 180 push-ups across four rounds.

The pull-up progression from L-pull-ups through chest-to-bar to standard kipping pull-ups creates a natural difficulty ladder within each round. The L-pull-ups demand the most core control; chest-to-bar demands the highest point of contact; the 20 standard pull-ups close the round at the highest volume. Push-ups bridge the pull-up variations, keeping the upper body under continuous tension throughout.

This is a grip-endurance and pulling-capacity test above all else. By round three, even experienced athletes will be breaking pull-up sets significantly. The push-ups, while easier than the pull-ups, accumulate quickly — 45 per round means 180 total. Never go to failure on push-ups in early rounds.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Weaver honors First Lieutenant Todd W. Weaver, 101st Airborne Division, who died on September 9, 2010, of wounds suffered in a roadside bomb attack in Kandahar, Afghanistan, at age 26.", "question": "Who is Weaver named after?"}, {"answer": "Weaver totals 360 reps: 180 pull-ups (across three variations) and 180 push-ups across four rounds.", "question": "What are the total reps in Weaver?"}, {"answer": "Each round goes: 10 L-pull-ups, 15 push-ups, 15 chest-to-bar pull-ups, 15 push-ups, 20 pull-ups, 15 push-ups — always returning to push-ups between pull-up variations.", "question": "What is the pull-up order in each round?"}, {"answer": "Weaver is a long pulling workout. Most athletes take 35–55 minutes to complete all four rounds. Scaling the pull-up variations appropriately is key to finishing in a reasonable time.", "question": "How long does Weaver take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'weaver';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'weaver'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Rahoi honors FBI Supervisory Special Agent Gregory J. Rahoi, 38, of Brookfield, Wisconsin, a member of the FBI Hostage Rescue Team based in Quantico, Virginia. He was killed on December 6, 2006, during a live-fire tactical training exercise at Fort A.P. Hill, near Bowling Green, Virginia. He is survived by his parents, Natalie and Richard; his sister, Teri; and his fiancee, Paula Paulk.

The Rahoi workout is a 12-minute AMRAP of 12 box jumps (24/20 inches), 6 thrusters (95/65 pounds), and 6 bar-facing burpees. It is a fast-moving triplet that rewards athletes who can stay smooth and efficient across all three movements.

The thruster is the anchor movement — it is the most demanding per rep, combining a front squat and push press into one explosive motion. At 95/65 pounds, the weight is manageable for most CrossFit athletes, but the accumulation across rounds will make it feel heavier as the clock winds down.

Twelve minutes is short enough that you should be working hard from the first minute. This is not a workout to pace conservatively — it demands sustained high output. Box jumps set the cardio dial; step down from the box to protect your Achilles and save your legs. Thrusters should be done in big sets or unbroken whenever possible. Burpees are your active rest — keep a consistent cadence rather than surging and stopping.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Rahoi honors FBI Supervisory Special Agent Gregory J. Rahoi, 38, of the Hostage Rescue Team, who was killed on December 6, 2006, during a live-fire tactical training exercise at Fort A.P. Hill, Virginia.", "question": "Who is Rahoi named after?"}, {"answer": "A bar-facing burpee is performed facing the loaded barbell. You perform a burpee and then jump laterally over the bar at the top. The jump over the bar counts as the rep completion.", "question": "What is a bar-facing burpee?"}, {"answer": "Rahoi is scalable for beginners with reduced thruster weight and box height. The 12-minute time cap makes it accessible — scale the movements so you can keep moving for the full duration.", "question": "Is Rahoi a good workout for beginners?"}, {"answer": "6+ rounds is a strong performance. Track your round count at the 6-minute mark and aim to match that pace in the second half.", "question": "How many rounds should I aim for?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'rahoi';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'rahoi'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Wilmot honors Canadian Forces Private Colin Wilmot, 24, of Fredericton, New Brunswick, who died on July 6, 2008, from wounds suffered when an explosive device detonated near him in the Panjwaii District of Afghanistan. Pte Wilmot was assigned to the 2nd Battalion, Princess Patricia''s Canadian Light Infantry (2 PPCLI) Battle Group, based out of Edmonton, Alberta. He is survived by his fiancee, Laura; his father, Eric Craig; and his sister, Kathleen. Wilmot is one of the few CrossFit hero workouts dedicated to honoring a fallen Canadian soldier.

The Wilmot workout is six rounds for time of 50 air squats and 25 ring dips. It is a pure lower-body and pressing volume test — 300 air squats and 150 ring dips — demanding exceptional tricep and chest endurance alongside quad and hip stamina.

Ring dips are significantly harder than bar dips. The unstable rings require constant shoulder stabilization on every rep, engaging the rotator cuff and serratus anterior throughout the movement. As fatigue sets in across rounds three through six, the rings will wobble more, making the movement feel exponentially harder than early rounds. The pressing muscles — triceps, chest, and shoulders — are under continuous load across 150 total ring dips.

Air squats between dips provide relative lower-body recovery while the upper body resets. Use the squat sets to breathe, swing out your arms, and mentally prepare for the next dip set. This is a workout where consistent sets and steady pacing dramatically outperform early aggression followed by singles.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Wilmot honors Canadian Forces Private Colin Wilmot, 24, of Fredericton, New Brunswick, who died on July 6, 2008, from wounds suffered when an explosive device detonated near him in the Panjwaii District of Afghanistan. It is one of the few CrossFit hero WODs honoring a Canadian soldier.", "question": "Who is Wilmot named after?"}, {"answer": "Ring dips require constant stabilization of the rings throughout the movement. The instability demands additional muscle activation in the shoulders, chest, and core compared to a fixed bar.", "question": "Why are ring dips harder than bar dips?"}, {"answer": "Wilmot totals 450 reps: 300 air squats and 150 ring dips across six rounds.", "question": "What are the total reps in Wilmot?"}, {"answer": "For athletes comfortable with ring dips, Wilmot takes approximately 25–40 minutes. Scaling ring dips to bar dips significantly reduces time.", "question": "How long does Wilmot take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'wilmot';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'wilmot'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Danny honors Oakland Police SWAT Sergeant Daniel Sakai, 35, who was killed in the line of duty on March 21, 2009, along with fellow officers Sgt. Ervin Romans, Sgt. Mark Dunakin and Officer John Hege. He is survived by his wife, Jenni, and his daughter, Jojiye.

A 20-minute AMRAP of 30 box jumps (24/20 inch), 20 push presses (115/75 lb), and 30 pull-ups — a high-rep triplet that demands consistent power output across all three movements. The round size is substantial: 80 reps per complete cycle means Danny favors athletes with efficient movement patterns and mental durability. Box jumps drive heart rate and leg fatigue; push press at 115 lb for 20 reps is demanding shoulder and tricep cycling; 30 pull-ups close every round with grip and pulling endurance. Three or more rounds is a solid benchmark.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Oakland Police SWAT Sergeant Daniel Sakai, 35, killed in the line of duty on March 21, 2009, along with fellow officers Sgt. Ervin Romans, Sgt. Mark Dunakin and Officer John Hege.", "question": "Who was Daniel Sakai?"}, {"answer": "3+ complete rounds is strong. 4 rounds is advanced. Elite athletes push 5 rounds in 20 minutes.", "question": "What is a good score for Danny?"}, {"answer": "115 lb for men, 75 lb for women.", "question": "What weight is the push press in Danny?"}, {"answer": "30 box jumps + 20 push presses + 30 pull-ups = 80 reps per complete round.", "question": "How many total reps per round in Danny?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'danny';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'danny'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Abbate honors USMC Sergeant Matthew T. Abbate, 26, of Honolulu, Hawaii, assigned to the 3rd Battalion, 5th Marine Regiment, 1st Marine Division, who was killed on December 2, 2010, while conducting combat operations in Helmand Province, Afghanistan. He was posthumously awarded the Navy Cross for his actions near Sangin two months earlier. He is survived by his wife, Stacie; his son, Carson; his parents; and his siblings.

The workout is a run-heavy test with two sets of 21 heavy clean-and-jerks (155 lb men / 105 lb women) sandwiched between three runs totaling about 2.5 miles: 1 mile, 800m, 1 mile. The heavy barbell weight — heavier than most hero WODs — makes the C&J sets the limiting factor. Most athletes complete each barbell set in multiple sub-sets, managing grip and shoulder fatigue between runs. The opening mile sets the tone: too fast and the first 21 C&Js become a grind; paced correctly and each barbell set flows. Sub-40 minutes is a strong performance.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Sub-40 minutes is strong. First-timers should aim to complete all runs and C&J sets without time pressure.", "question": "What is a good time for Abbate?"}, {"answer": "155 lb for men, 105 lb for women — heavier than most hero WODs. Any style of clean and jerk is permitted.", "question": "How heavy are the clean-and-jerks in Abbate?"}, {"answer": "1 mile + 800m + 1 mile = about 2.5 miles total, interspersed with two sets of 21 clean-and-jerks.", "question": "What is the total distance run in Abbate?"}, {"answer": "USMC Sergeant Matthew T. Abbate, 26, of the 3rd Battalion, 5th Marines, killed on December 2, 2010, while conducting combat operations in Helmand Province, Afghanistan. He was posthumously awarded the Navy Cross.", "question": "Who is Abbate named after?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'abbate';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'abbate'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set faqs = '[{"answer": "Sub-35 minutes is elite. 40–50 minutes is solid. The Seven is one of the most demanding hero WODs — finishing under any reasonable cap is a victory.", "question": "What is a good The Seven time?"}, {"answer": "The Seven honors seven CIA personnel killed by a suicide bomber at a remote base in southeastern Afghanistan on Dec. 30, 2009: CIA officers Jennifer Lynne Matthews, Scott Michael Roberson, Harold E. Brown Jr., Darren LaBonte and Elizabeth Hanson, and security contractors Jeremy Jason Wise and Dane Clark Paresi.", "question": "Who is The Seven named after?"}, {"answer": "Treat it like a long-distance run — slow and steady. Athletes who go out hard in round 1 typically collapse by round 4. Break every movement into small sets from the beginning.", "question": "How should I pace The Seven?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'the-seven';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'the-seven'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'War Frank honors Captain Warren A. Frank, United States Marine Corps, 26, of Cincinnati, Ohio, who died on November 25, 2008, while supporting combat operations in Ninewa Province, Iraq. Capt. Frank was assigned to the 5th Air Naval Gunfire Liaison Company, III Marine Expeditionary Force, Okinawa, Japan. He is survived by his wife, Allison, and his daughters, Sophia Lynn and Isabella Grace.

The War Frank workout is three rounds for time of 25 muscle-ups, 100 air squats, and 35 GHD sit-ups. The sheer volume of muscle-ups — 75 total across three rounds — makes this one of the most gymnastics-demanding hero workouts in the CrossFit canon.

Twenty-five muscle-ups per round is an extraordinary ask. Even elite athletes will need to break these into multiple sets. The transition from the swing to the dip is a skill point that degrades sharply under fatigue — by round two, many athletes are grinding through singles. Plan your sets before you start: 8+7+6+4 or 5×5 is far smarter than going for large unbroken sets that leave you hanging.

The 100 air squats serve as active recovery after the muscle-up volume, allowing your grip and shoulders a chance to decompress before the GHD sit-ups. GHDs then challenge the posterior chain and hip flexors — a significant core fatigue point before you return to round two of muscle-ups. Move through the squats and GHDs at a consistent pace; they are not the limiting factor here.', faqs = '[{"answer": "War Frank honors Marine Captain Warren A. Frank, 26, of the 5th Air Naval Gunfire Liaison Company, who died on November 25, 2008, while supporting combat operations in Ninewa Province, Iraq.", "question": "Who is War Frank named after?"}, {"answer": "War Frank includes 75 total muscle-ups (25 per round across 3 rounds), making it one of the highest-volume muscle-up workouts in CrossFit.", "question": "How many total muscle-ups are in War Frank?"}, {"answer": "Yes — scale to 15 per round or substitute ring rows + ring dips at equal or higher reps. War Frank is one of the few workouts where scaling is almost always necessary.", "question": "Can I substitute muscle-ups?"}, {"answer": "For athletes who can do muscle-ups, War Frank takes 30–60 minutes depending on gymnastics proficiency. The muscle-up volume is the dominant time factor.", "question": "How long does War Frank take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'war-frank';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'war-frank'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Badger honors U.S. Navy Chief Petty Officer Mark Carter, 27, of Fallbrook, California, a Navy SEAL who was killed during combat operations in Iraq on December 11, 2007. Badger was the nickname his fellow SEALs gave him. He is survived by his father, Tom; his mother, Cindy; three brothers; and four sisters.

Three rounds of 30 squat cleans (95/65 lb), 30 pull-ups, and an 800m run — 90 squat cleans, 90 pull-ups, and 2,400 meters of running in total. The squat clean at 95/65 lb demands both technique and leg endurance across 30 reps; the pull-ups punish grip accumulated from the barbell; the 800m run is long enough to serve as recovery but challenging enough after 60 demanding reps. The triplet structure makes each round a complete workout on its own. By round three, every station feels significantly harder than it did in round one. Sub-40 minutes is a strong performance.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Navy SEAL Chief Petty Officer Mark Carter, 27, of Fallbrook, California, killed during combat operations in Iraq on December 11, 2007. Badger was his nickname among fellow SEALs.", "question": "Who is Badger named after?"}, {"answer": "Sub-40 minutes is strong. Sub-35 minutes is advanced. The combination of 90 squat cleans, 90 pull-ups, and 2,400 meters of running makes this one of the longer hero WODs.", "question": "What is a good time for Badger?"}, {"answer": "95 lb for men, 65 lb for women — a moderate weight that is challenging at 30 reps per round.", "question": "What weight are the squat cleans in Badger?"}, {"answer": "90 squat cleans + 90 pull-ups + 2,400m of running across three rounds.", "question": "How many total reps are in Badger?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'badger';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'badger'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Jerry honors U.S. Army Sergeant Major Jerry Dwayne Patton, 40, who died on October 15, 2008, during High Altitude High Opening (HAHO) parachute training. He was assigned to Army USSOCOM and was preparing for deployment to Afghanistan. He is survived by his wife, Molly, and his sons, Chad, Cody, Chase and Connor.

A pure aerobic test: 1 mile run, 2,000m row, 1 mile run. No barbells, no gymnastics — just cardiovascular capacity and the ability to maintain output across two different machines. The 2,000m row separates the runs and represents a significant cardiovascular and muscular demand in its own right. Athletes who run their first mile too fast arrive at the rower already compromised. The second mile after 2,000m of rowing tests whether any reserves remain. The best athletes finish under 22 minutes; strong performances are sub-25.', faqs = '[{"answer": "U.S. Army Sergeant Major Jerry Dwayne Patton, 40, who died on October 15, 2008, during High Altitude High Opening (HAHO) training while preparing for deployment to Afghanistan.", "question": "Who was Jerry Patton?"}, {"answer": "Sub-22 minutes is strong. Sub-20 minutes is elite. Most athletes finish in 22-28 minutes.", "question": "What is a good time for Jerry?"}, {"answer": "1 mile run → 2,000m row → 1 mile run. All three for time.", "question": "What is the order of movements in Jerry?"}, {"answer": "Each discipline uses different primary muscles. The row fatigues the legs and back while allowing the running muscles partial recovery; the final mile tests whether those running muscles have recovered enough after 2,000m of pulling.", "question": "Why is the run-row-run structure so challenging?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'jerry';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'jerry'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Jack honors U.S. Army Staff Sgt. Jack M. Martin III, 26, of Bethany, Oklahoma, assigned to the 3rd Battalion, 1st Special Forces Group, who died on Sept. 29, 2009, on Jolo Island, Philippines, from the detonation of an improvised explosive device. A fitting tribute of pressing, swinging, and jumping, it is a fast-paced triplet that rewards consistent pacing over the full 20 minutes.', faqs = '[{"answer": "15+ rounds is elite. 10 to 14 rounds is solid. 7 to 9 rounds is a good result for most athletes.", "question": "What is a good Jack score?"}, {"answer": "Light enough for unbroken sets of 10 every round. If you have to break up the push presses before round 5, reduce the weight.", "question": "How heavy should the push press be in Jack?"}, {"answer": "Jack honors U.S. Army Staff Sgt. Jack M. Martin III, 26, of the 3rd Battalion, 1st Special Forces Group, who died on Sept. 29, 2009, on Jolo Island, Philippines. It is a memorial hero WOD honoring his sacrifice.", "question": "Who was Jack?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'jack';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'jack'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'RJ honors Sergeant Major Robert J. Cottle, a veteran Los Angeles Police Department officer and U.S. Marine Corps Reservist, who was killed by an improvised explosive device while on patrol in southern Afghanistan on March 24, 2010, at age 45. Known as "RJ," he joined the Marines at 18 and the LAPD in 1990, and most recently served on the LAPD SWAT team. He is survived by his wife, Emily, and his daughter, Kaila.

The RJ workout is a five-round chipper of an 800-meter run, 5 rope climbs (15-foot rope), and 50 push-ups. It combines aerobic endurance with climbing capacity and raw push-up volume in one of the most demanding upper-body hero workouts in the canon.

Five rope climbs per round — 25 total — is what makes RJ exceptional. Each climb demands grip strength, lat activation, and a coordinated leg wrap. In round one they feel manageable; by round four, your hands and forearms will be worked to their limit. The 50 push-ups follow immediately after the rope climbs, compounding the fatigue on your pushing muscles.

The 800-meter run gives your grip and shoulders a brief recovery window, but do not blow out the run. Treat it as a strong, sustainable effort — about 75–80% — so you step back to the rope ready to climb. Pacing the rope climbs is essential: break them into singles with full rest early rather than grinding through multiple climbs until you miss a grip.', faqs = '[{"answer": "RJ honors veteran LAPD officer and Marine Corps Reservist Sgt. Maj. Robert J. Cottle, known as ''RJ,'' who was killed by an improvised explosive device while on patrol in southern Afghanistan on March 24, 2010, at age 45.", "question": "Who is RJ named after?"}, {"answer": "RJ includes 5 rope climbs per round for 5 rounds — 25 total rope climbs. This makes it one of the highest-volume climbing workouts in CrossFit.", "question": "How many total rope climbs are in RJ?"}, {"answer": "Yes — the standard is a 15-foot rope climb from standing. If your rope is shorter or you''re scaling, count reps accordingly.", "question": "Do I need to climb 15 feet?"}, {"answer": "RJ is a long workout. Most athletes take 45–70 minutes to complete all five rounds. Plan for a full training session and scale rope climbs generously.", "question": "How long does RJ take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'rj';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'rj'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set faqs = '[{"answer": "DT is a hero workout consisting of 5 rounds for time of: 12 deadlifts, 9 hang power cleans, and 6 push jerks, all at 155 lb (70 kg) for men and 105 lb (48 kg) for women. It honors U.S. Air Force Staff Sgt. Timothy P. Davis, 28, who was killed on Feb. 20, 2009, while supporting Operation Enduring Freedom, when his vehicle was struck by an improvised explosive device.", "question": "What is the DT workout?"}, {"answer": "Sub-20 min is strong.", "question": "What is a good DT time?"}, {"answer": "Rx weight for DT is 155 lb (70 kg) for men and 105 lb (48 kg) for women. This is a moderately heavy barbell weight that requires efficient cycling. Athletes should be comfortable with 15+ unbroken deadlifts at this weight before attempting Rx.", "question": "How heavy is DT?"}, {"answer": "Yes, and ideally you should. DT is designed to be done touch-and-go: deadlifts flow into hang power cleans (bar stays above the knee), then into push jerks. Setting the bar down adds significant time over 5 rounds.", "question": "Can I do DT without setting the bar down?"}, {"answer": "DT targets the posterior chain heavily (deadlifts and hang cleans: glutes, hamstrings, lower back), the traps and shoulders (clean and jerk), and the full body through the barbell cycling demand.", "question": "What muscles does DT work?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'dt';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'dt'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Helton honors U.S. Air Force Security Forces 1st Lieutenant Joseph D. Helton, 24, of Monroe, Georgia, who was killed on September 8, 2009, while on a mission near Baghdad, Iraq, when an improvised explosive device detonated near his vehicle. He was assigned to the 6th Security Forces Squadron at MacDill Air Force Base in Tampa, Florida, and is survived by his mother, Jiffy.

Three rounds of an 800m run, 30 dumbbell squat cleans (50/35 lb each), and 30 burpees — 90 squat cleans, 90 burpees, and 2.4km of running across three rounds. The dumbbell squat clean at 50/35 lb each is demanding: receiving two heavy dumbbells in a full squat requires hip mobility, leg strength, and coordination. Thirty per round creates significant cumulative lower body and grip fatigue. The burpees following each squat clean set tax the shoulder and chest with what little pressing capacity remains before the next run. The runs serve as active recovery — but three 800m runs are demanding on their own.', faqs = '[{"answer": "U.S. Air Force 1st Lt. Joseph D. Helton, 24, of the 6th Security Forces Squadron, killed by an IED near Baghdad, Iraq, on September 8, 2009.", "question": "Who was Joseph Helton?"}, {"answer": "Sub-35 minutes is strong. Sub-30 minutes is advanced. The combination of heavy dumbbell squat cleans and burpees makes this a demanding workout with a typical finish time of 30-45 minutes.", "question": "What is a good time for Helton?"}, {"answer": "50 lb each hand for men, 35 lb each hand for women.", "question": "What weight are the dumbbell squat cleans in Helton?"}, {"answer": "90 dumbbell squat cleans + 90 burpees + 2,400m of running.", "question": "What is the total volume in Helton?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'helton';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'helton'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Jason honors Navy SEAL Special Warfare Operator First Class Jason Dale Lewis, who was killed by an IED while conducting combat operations in southern Baghdad on July 6, 2007. He is survived by his wife, Donna, and his children, Jack, Max and Grace. A descending squat ladder paired with an ascending muscle-up ladder: 100 air squats then 5 muscle-ups, 75 squats then 10, 50 squats then 15, 25 squats then 20 — for time.

The anti-correlation of the two ladders is the defining feature: as the squat volume decreases, the muscle-up demand increases. Legs fill with lactic acid in the first two rounds; arms and shoulders take over as the primary demand in rounds three and four. By the final round of 20 muscle-ups, athletes arrive with the least leg fatigue but the most accumulated upper-body fatigue. Athletes who do not have muscle-ups will spend enormous time on the gymnastics stations. Total muscle-up volume is 50 reps — one of the higher MU counts in any CrossFit benchmark.', updated_at = now() where slug = 'jason';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'jason'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Bradshaw honors U.S. Army 1st Lieutenant Brian Bradshaw, 24, of Steilacoom, Washington, killed in action on June 25, 2009, in Kheyl, Afghanistan, when an improvised explosive device detonated near his vehicle. He was assigned to the 1st Battalion, 501st Parachute Infantry Regiment, and is survived by his parents, Paul and Mary, and his brother, Robert.

Ten rounds of 3 handstand push-ups, 6 deadlifts (225/155 lb), 12 pull-ups, and 24 double-unders — a workout that keeps you moving without extended rest at any station. The HSPU-to-deadlift transition is the defining pairing: pressing while inverted followed immediately by a heavy hip hinge creates a total upper-body demand within each round. Twelve pull-ups extend the grip and pulling chain. Twenty-four double-unders at the end of each round test jump rope consistency when arms and shoulders are pre-fatigued. Ten rounds compound all of it. Sub-25 minutes is strong.', updated_at = now() where slug = 'bradshaw';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'bradshaw'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Johnson honors U.S. Marine Corps 1st Lieutenant Michael E. Johnson, 25, of the 7th Communications Battalion, 3rd Marine Headquarters Group, III Marine Expeditionary Force, headquartered in Okinawa, Japan. He died on September 8, 2009, while supporting combat operations in Kunar Province, Afghanistan, and is survived by his wife, Durinda.

A 20-minute AMRAP of 9 deadlifts (245/165 lb), 8 muscle-ups, and 9 squat cleans (155/105 lb) — one of the most demanding AMRAPs in the hero library. The barbell weights are heavy: 245 lb deadlifts for 9 reps create immediate posterior chain fatigue; 155 lb squat cleans demand both hip power and receiving position strength. The 8 muscle-ups between the two barbell movements require upper-body pulling capacity after grip has been taxed by the deadlift. Athletes who complete 3+ rounds are in elite territory. The workout is unforgiving from the first rep.', faqs = '[{"answer": "USMC 1st Lt. Michael E. Johnson, 25, of the 7th Communications Battalion, III Marine Expeditionary Force, who died on September 8, 2009, while supporting combat operations in Kunar Province, Afghanistan.", "question": "Who was Michael Johnson?"}, {"answer": "3+ complete rounds (78+ reps) is an advanced performance. 4 rounds (104 reps) is elite. The heavy barbell weights and muscle-ups make each round take 4-7 minutes.", "question": "What is a good score for Johnson?"}, {"answer": "Deadlifts: 245 lb men / 165 lb women. Squat cleans: 155 lb men / 105 lb women.", "question": "What weight are the barbell movements in Johnson?"}, {"answer": "Both ring and bar muscle-ups count. Athletes should use whichever style they are stronger in.", "question": "Can you use ring or bar muscle-ups in Johnson?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'johnson';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'johnson'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Small honors Staff Sergeant Marc Small, United States Army Special Forces, 29, of Collegeville, Pennsylvania, who died on February 12, 2009, from wounds sustained when insurgents attacked his unit with a rocket-propelled grenade launcher and small-arms fire in Faramuz, Afghanistan. SSG Small was assigned to the 1st Battalion, 3rd Special Forces Group (Airborne), Fort Bragg, North Carolina. He is survived by his father and stepmother, his mother and stepfather, and his fiancee, Amanda Charney.

The Small workout is three rounds for time of a 1,000-meter row, 50 burpees, 50 box jumps (24/20 inches), and an 800-meter run. It is one of the longest and most cardiovascularly demanding hero workouts in the CrossFit library — testing sustained aerobic capacity, mental fortitude, and movement efficiency across a massive volume of work.

Each round is effectively its own brutal mini-workout: row, grind through burpees and box jumps, then run. Three rounds means you face the same mountain of work with no reprieve. The 1,000-meter row sets the cardiovascular tone for the round — push it too hard and the 50 burpees will punish you immediately. Go out controlled on the row, settle into a steady pace on burpees and box jumps, and then push the 800-meter run.

Burpees in sets of 50 require a strong mental strategy: pick a rhythm and commit to it. Do not stop and stare at the floor — set a cadence of one rep every 5–6 seconds and maintain it. Box jumps can be stepped up and stepped down to preserve leg endurance for the run.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Small honors Staff Sergeant Marc Small, 1st Battalion, 3rd Special Forces Group (Airborne), who died on February 12, 2009, from wounds sustained in an insurgent attack in Faramuz, Afghanistan, at age 29.", "question": "Who is Small named after?"}, {"answer": "Small is one of the longest hero WODs. Most athletes take 60–90 minutes. Scale generously to keep it under 75 minutes.", "question": "How long does Small take?"}, {"answer": "Small totals 5,400 meters of movement: 3,000m rowing and 2,400m running across three rounds.", "question": "What is the total distance in Small?"}, {"answer": "Excluding the row and run, Small includes 150 burpees and 150 box jumps — 300 total reps of calisthenics across three rounds.", "question": "What is the total rep count?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'small';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'small'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Tyler honors First Lieutenant Tyler E. Parten, United States Army, 24, of Arkansas, who died on September 10, 2009, in Konar Province, Afghanistan, of wounds sustained when insurgents attacked his unit using rocket-propelled grenades and small-arms fire. 1LT Parten was assigned to the 3rd Squadron, 61st Cavalry Regiment, 4th Brigade Combat Team, 4th Infantry Division, Fort Carson, Colorado. He is survived by his mother, Lona, and his brother, Daniel.

The Tyler workout is five rounds for time of 7 muscle-ups and 21 sumo deadlift high-pulls (95/65 pounds). It is a powerful couplet that pairs the most demanding upper-body gymnastics movement with a full-body barbell pull — testing both gymnastics capacity and hip-driven power in equal measure.

Seven muscle-ups per round (35 total) is a significant gymnastics ask. The muscle-up demands a coordinated kip, a fast transition through the rings, and a controlled lockout above — all of which degrade as fatigue builds across five rounds. The sumo deadlift high-pull at 95 pounds follows each muscle-up set, demanding that the shoulders and upper back continue working after ring work.

The SDHP is a wide-stance pull from the floor to chin height — it is a full-body movement powered primarily by the hips, with the arms completing the pull. The combination with muscle-ups creates a pulling-heavy workout that will challenge your lats, biceps, and grip across all five rounds. Pacing both movements from the start is essential.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Tyler honors First Lieutenant Tyler E. Parten, 3rd Squadron, 61st Cavalry Regiment, 4th Infantry Division, who died on September 10, 2009, in Konar Province, Afghanistan, at age 24.", "question": "Who is Tyler named after?"}, {"answer": "A sumo deadlift high-pull (SDHP) starts in a wide sumo stance with hands inside the knees on the bar. The movement is a hip-driven pull from the floor to chin height, with the elbows flaring high and wide at the top.", "question": "What is a sumo deadlift high-pull?"}, {"answer": "Tyler totals 140 reps: 35 muscle-ups and 105 sumo deadlift high-pulls across five rounds.", "question": "How many total reps are in Tyler?"}, {"answer": "The combination of 35 total muscle-ups and heavy pulling volume with SDHPs creates extreme lat, bicep, and grip fatigue. Most athletes find the muscle-up capacity in later rounds is the primary limiting factor.", "question": "What makes Tyler difficult compared to other hero WODs?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'tyler';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'tyler'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Hammer honors U.S. Army 1st Sergeant Michael "Hammer" Bordelon, 37, of Morgan City, Louisiana, who died on May 10, 2005, from injuries sustained when a car bomb exploded near him in Mosul, Iraq, on April 23, 2005. 1SG Bordelon was assigned to the 1st Battalion, 24th Infantry Regiment, 1st Brigade, 25th Infantry Division (Stryker Brigade Combat Team), based at Fort Lewis, Washington. He is survived by his wife, Mila; his children, Mike Jr., Jacob and Johanna; his mother, Dolores; and his sister, Doreen.

Five rounds, each timed independently with 90 seconds of mandatory rest between rounds: 5 power cleans, 10 front squats, 5 push jerks, and 20 pull-ups — all at 135/95 lb. The barbell complex flows naturally from clean to squat to jerk; the 20 pull-ups close each round with grip and pulling demand. The mandatory 90-second rest means each round is a sub-max sprint followed by partial recovery — a format that rewards athletes who can surge effectively. By rounds 4 and 5, the accumulated fatigue from the barbell cycling and pull-ups makes what felt easy in round 1 feel genuinely challenging.', faqs = '[{"answer": "U.S. Army 1st Sergeant Michael \"Hammer\" Bordelon, 37, of the 25th Infantry Division, who died on May 10, 2005, from injuries sustained when a car bomb exploded near him in Mosul, Iraq.", "question": "Who was Michael Bordelon?"}, {"answer": "Each of the five rounds is timed individually. The total score is typically the cumulative time including rest periods, or the sum of all round times.", "question": "How is Hammer scored?"}, {"answer": "Yes — the rest is built into the workout structure. Do not start early. It is part of what makes the format challenging.", "question": "Is the 90-second rest mandatory in Hammer?"}, {"answer": "135 lb for men, 95 lb for women — used for power cleans, front squats, and push jerks across all five rounds.", "question": "What weight is the barbell in Hammer?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'hammer';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'hammer'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Hortman honors U.S. Army Captain John D. Hortman, 30, of Inman, South Carolina, who died on August 8, 2011, in a helicopter accident during military training at Fort Benning, Georgia. Capt. Hortman was assigned to the 1st Battalion, 160th Special Operations Aviation Regiment, based at Fort Campbell, Kentucky, and is survived by his mother, Brenda Jones; his sister, Jill; and his brother, Andy Pierce.

A 45-minute AMRAP of 800m run, 80 air squats, and 8 muscle-ups — entirely bodyweight, entirely demanding. The muscle-up is the separator: 8 per round across what might be 5-6 rounds means 40-48 muscle-ups total for strong athletes, combined with 4-5 miles of running and 400-480 air squats. The 45-minute format is longer than most AMRAPs, demanding sustained pacing across a genuinely long effort. Athletes who do not have muscle-ups will score zero rounds — the scaled version substitutes a more accessible gymnastics movement. Completing 4+ rounds is a strong performance.', faqs = '[{"answer": "U.S. Army Captain John D. Hortman, 30, killed in a helicopter accident at Fort Benning on August 8, 2011. He served with the 1st Battalion, 160th Special Operations Aviation Regiment.", "question": "Who was John Hortman?"}, {"answer": "4+ rounds in 45 minutes is a strong performance. 5-6 rounds requires elite conditioning and muscle-up proficiency. Completing 3 rounds is solid for intermediate athletes.", "question": "What is a good score for Hortman?"}, {"answer": "3.2 km of running (4 x 800m) + 320 air squats (4 x 80) + 32 muscle-ups (4 x 8).", "question": "What is the total volume in a 4-round performance of Hortman?"}, {"answer": "CrossFit does not explain the time domain. The 45 minutes make Hortman a workout where pacing strategy matters as much as raw fitness.", "question": "Why is Hortman 45 minutes instead of the typical 20-minute AMRAP?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'hortman';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'hortman'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Coe honors U.S. Army Sgt. Keith Adam Coe, 30, of Auburndale, Florida, who died on April 27, 2010, in Khalis, Iraq, of wounds sustained when enemy forces attacked his unit with an explosive device. He was assigned to the 1st Battalion, 37th Field Artillery Regiment, 3rd Stryker Brigade Combat Team, 2nd Infantry Division, and is survived by his wife, Katrina; his sons, Killian and Keith Jr.; and his daughter, Klover. The workout is 10 rounds of 10 thrusters and 10 ring push-ups. That adds up to 100 thrusters at 95/65 lb, the same weight as Fran, and 100 ring push-ups.

The two movements hit the same muscles from different angles. Thrusters tax the legs, lungs and shoulders, and ring push-ups make the chest, triceps and shoulders work while the rings move under you. Neither is hard for one round. The challenge is holding steady, repeatable sets for ten rounds while the pressing fatigue builds. Under 12 minutes is a strong time; most athletes finish in 12 to 20.', updated_at = now() where slug = 'coe';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'coe'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Coffey honors USMC Corporal Keaton G. Coffey, 22, of Boring, Oregon, who was killed on May 24, 2012, while conducting combat operations in Helmand Province, Afghanistan. Cpl. Coffey served with the 1st Law Enforcement Battalion, 1st Marine Headquarters Group, I Marine Expeditionary Force, and is survived by his fiancee, Brittany Dygert, and his parents, Grant and Inger.

A descending chipper with four 800m runs separating each barbell section: 800m run, 50 back squats (135/95 lb) + 50 bench press (135/95 lb), 800m run, 35 of each, 800m run, 20 of each, 800m run, and 1 muscle-up. Total: 105 back squats, 105 bench presses, and 3.2 km of running with a single muscle-up as the final statement. The genius of this structure is how each run arrives on legs fatigued by squats, and each bench press set arrives on arms still pumped from running. The single muscle-up at the very end requires composure and technique after everything else.', faqs = '[{"answer": "USMC Corporal Keaton G. Coffey, 22, of Boring, Oregon, killed on May 24, 2012, while conducting combat operations in Helmand Province, Afghanistan. He served with the 1st Law Enforcement Battalion, I Marine Expeditionary Force.", "question": "Who was Keaton Coffey?"}, {"answer": "105 back squats + 105 bench presses + 3,200m of running + 1 muscle-up.", "question": "What is the total volume in Coffey?"}, {"answer": "The single muscle-up after 3.2 km of running and 210 barbell reps is a final test of composure and technique. It requires one clean, focused rep when you have almost nothing left.", "question": "Why does Coffey end with a single muscle-up?"}, {"answer": "135 lb for men, 95 lb for women — the same weight for both back squats and bench press throughout all rounds.", "question": "What weight are the barbell movements in Coffey?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'coffey';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'coffey'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set description = 'Hotshots 19 honors the 19 members of the Granite Mountain Hotshots firefighting crew who lost their lives fighting the Yarnell Hill Fire in Yarnell, Arizona, on June 30, 2013. It was the largest loss of U.S. firefighters in a single event since Sept. 11, 2001. The fallen were: Andrew Ashcraft, Robert Caldwell, Travis Carter, Dustin Deford, Christopher MacKenzie, Eric Marsh, Grant McKee, Sean Misner, Scott Norris, Wade Parker, John Percin, Anthony Rose, Jesse Steed, Joe Thurston, Travis Turbyfill, William Warneke, Clayton Whitted, Kevin Woyjeck, and Garret Zuppiger.

The workout is 6 rounds for time of 30 air squats, 19 power cleans (135/95 pounds), 7 strict pull-ups, and a 400-meter run. Every 19 reps in this workout directly honor the 19 fallen crew members. It is commonly performed on or near June 30 each year, and has become one of the most emotionally resonant memorial WODs in the CrossFit community.

The power clean weight is demanding across 6 rounds — 114 total power cleans at 135 pounds require strong hip mechanics and careful pulling technique as fatigue builds. Strict pull-ups add upper-body pulling volume between barbell work and running, targeting the lats and biceps on an already tired upper body.

Air squats open each round and provide a relative lower-body warm-up before the barbell. The 400-meter run closes each round with a cardiovascular flush. This workout is long — plan for 35–55 minutes — and should be approached with consistent pacing from the very first round.', faqs = '[{"answer": "Hotshots 19 honors the 19 members of the Granite Mountain Hotshots who lost their lives fighting the Yarnell Hill Fire in Yarnell, Arizona, on June 30, 2013. It was the largest loss of U.S. firefighters in a single event since Sept. 11, 2001.", "question": "Who does Hotshots 19 honor?"}, {"answer": "Each set of 19 power cleans directly represents the 19 fallen crew members of the Granite Mountain Hotshots. The rep scheme is intentional and meaningful.", "question": "Why 19 power cleans per round?"}, {"answer": "Hotshots 19 is traditionally performed on or near June 30, the anniversary of the Yarnell Hill wildfire. Many CrossFit affiliates program it as a community memorial workout on that date.", "question": "When is Hotshots 19 traditionally performed?"}, {"answer": "Most athletes finish in 45–65 min. With appropriate scaling, plan for approximately 45 minutes of sustained effort.", "question": "How long does Hotshots 19 take?"}]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = 'hotshots-19';
  get diagnostics n = row_count; if n <> 1 then raise exception 'slug % not updated', 'hotshots-19'; end if;
  update benchmark_workouts set scaling_notes = replace(scaling_notes, 'Keep all four runs', 'Keep all three runs'), updated_at = now()
    where slug = 'daniel' and scaling_notes like '%Keep all four runs%';
